{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2166 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "98c8e74516d50770a6aac18192375a2d3176a15831af887a24c82891a1451b84" :edb/db-before-t 2165 :edb/db-after-t 2166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535478 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:53:57.701Z" 13194139535478 true] [17592186147317 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n1. $y''+y'+3y = t$\n2. $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n3. $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139535478 false] [17592186147317 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\n- $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186147317 1145 "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$.\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0$.\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$." 13194139535478 false] [17592186147317 1145 "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$\n\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0.$\n\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186147318 1100 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$, and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}$.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535478 false] [17592186147318 1100 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535478 true] [17592186147318 1145 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2\n$$" 13194139535478 false] [17592186147318 1145 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2.\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186147319 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\n- $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\n- $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139535478 true] [17592186147319 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y''+2y' + 5 y = 50t$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n2. $y = R e ^ {-t} \\cos \\left(2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n3. $y =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139535478 false] [17592186147319 1145 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t)$, which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) - 1 + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 1 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{c}(t) &= e^{-t}(A\\cos 2t + B\\sin 2t), \\\\\ny_{c}(t) &= Re^{-t}\\cos (2t + ϕ), \\\\\ny_{c}(t) &= Re^{-t}\\sin (2t + ϕ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t)$. Note that the external force\n\n$$\nf(t)=50t\n$$\n\nis a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4$. So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= e^{-t}(A\\cos 2t + B\\sin 2t) + 10t - 4, \\\\\ny(t) &= Re^{-t}\\cos (2t + ϕ) + 10t - 4, \\\\\ny(t) &= Re^{-t}\\sin (2t + ϕ) + 10t - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535478 false] [17592186147319 1145 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation $y''+2y' + 5 y = 0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t)=50t$ is a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535478 true] [17592186147320 1100 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + y =2e^{-t}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." 13194139535478 false] [17592186147320 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." 13194139535478 true] [17592186147320 1145 "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0$. So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t)$. Note that the external force\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2e^{-t}\n$$\n\nis an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{-t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1$. Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1$, and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1$.\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(π) &= -\\cos π + \\sin π + e^{- π} \\\\\n&= (1 + e^{- π})m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535478 false] [17592186147320 1145 "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0.$ So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1,\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t) = 2e^{-t}$ is an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha  e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1.$ Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1,$ and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1.$\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186147387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351172 13194139535478 true] [17592186147387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351234 13194139535478 true] [17592186147387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351241 13194139535478 true] [17592186147387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351248 13194139535478 true] [17592186147388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351179 13194139535478 true] [17592186147388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351186 13194139535478 true] [17592186147388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351193 13194139535478 true] [17592186147388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351255 13194139535478 true] [17592186147389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351213 13194139535478 true] [17592186147389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351220 13194139535478 true] [17592186147389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351269 13194139535478 true] [17592186147389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351276 13194139535478 true] [17592186147390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351200 13194139535478 true] [17592186147390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351227 13194139535478 true] [17592186147390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351262 13194139535478 true] [17592186148118 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535478 true] [17592186148118 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139535478 false] [17592186148118 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139535478 true] [17592186148118 1145 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186148119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535478 true] [17592186148119 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139535478 true] [17592186148119 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 9y = 10e^{-t}$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos (3t + ϕ) + e^{-t}$\n2. $y = R\\sin (t + ϕ) + e^{-2t}$\n3. $y = A\\cos 3t + B\\sin 3t + e^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139535478 false] [17592186148119 1145 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535478 true] [17592186148120 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535478 true] [17592186148120 1100 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535478 true] [17592186148120 1100 "A particle of mass $3kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15kg/s^{2}$.\nAn external force $F(t) = 9\\sin (t)N$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535478 false] [17592186148120 1145 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-15y+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186148125 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535478 true] [17592186148125 1100 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = 8t^{2}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." 13194139535478 false] [17592186148125 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=8{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." 13194139535478 true] [17592186148125 1145 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186148132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186146803 13194139535478 false] [17592186148132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351207 13194139535478 true] [17592186148132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535478 true] [17592186148132 1100 "The position $y(t)$, measured in inches, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 5y = 34\\cos 2t$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $π$ seconds." 13194139535478 false] [17592186148132 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in inches, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=34\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds." 13194139535478 true] [17592186148132 1145 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $34\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =8.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=-9.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-{e}^{-t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-{e}^{-\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =2(1-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351173 13194139535478 true] [17592186351172 1089 "q-64596" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535478 true] [17592186351172 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351172 1113 #uuid "37f9e5c6-cf26-5f3e-a7db-e8ef0cd04146" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351172 1145 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351174 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351175 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351176 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351177 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351178 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1041 "selection" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351174 13194139535478 true] [17592186351173 1110 #uuid "bd05d467-03a2-5d2a-abe7-4bb0f044904f" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351174 1034 "{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351174 1111 #uuid "84c14