3461b2b-1815-598a-951c-8a87c6354f5f" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351190 1034 "{y}^{″}+4.5y=6{t}^{3}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351190 1111 #uuid "e5f059f6-7204-537a-aabf-3ebcb7ea3f76" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351191 1034 "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351191 1111 #uuid "b9e7623c-4ed4-58d3-bc3d-a2516df5ccc2" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351192 1034 "{y}^{″}+4.5y=12{t}^{3}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351192 1111 #uuid "28c0080b-f44e-56e1-a0be-8cad184b1415" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351193 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351194 13194139535478 true] [17592186351193 1089 "q-64602" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351193 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535478 true] [17592186351193 1100 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=12\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=6{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351193 1113 #uuid "4e9a65e3-dfc8-5e26-8b20-c96df0dc09f5" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351193 1145 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-12{y}^{′}-15y+6{e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=2{e}^{4t}.\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351195 13194139535478 true] [175