ned by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." 13194139535478 true] [17592186351200 1113 #uuid "77ef26fc-689a-5c81-bebc-1ad78410e558" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351200 1145 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351202 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351203 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351204 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351205 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351206 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1041 "selection" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351205 13194139535478 true] [17592186351201 1110 #uuid "80bfdcc7-9bb2-55b4-9b22-eabfa9b1ffbb" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351202 1034 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(3+\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351202 1111 #uuid "b59beb70-3ecf-5195-8b3c-91c505c0f95c" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351203 1034 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351203 1111 #uuid "7e40abca-77c5-5180-ab48-de1fb14c43af" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351204 1034 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(2-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186351204 1111 #uuid "5eb3f8b5-d423-5a9f-8698-8255f6a2a7b7" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351205 1034 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535478 true] [17592186