194139535478 true] [17592186351212 1111 #uuid "11f4617b-0784-5289-8bca-ed5b6ef2bf1e" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351213 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351214 13194139535478 true] [17592186351213 1089 "q-79575" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351213 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535478 true] [17592186351213 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=40\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$\n- $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n- $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139535478 true] [17592186351213 1113 #uuid "5296c56b-6b80-5c29-a2da-85a2d42fa001" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351213 1145 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351215 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351216 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351217 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351218 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351219 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1041 "selection" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351217 13194139535478 true] [17592186351214 1110 #uuid "c0a57854-c16b-524e-a399-5a486e0d322d" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351215 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535478 true] [17592186351215 1111 #uuid "c6b4b27b-5d92-5b2c-9559-e899c58c2f7b" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351216 1034 "I only" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535478 true] [17592186351216 1111 #uuid "7c9a5277-25c6-51c9-9517-ca7df2243bea" 131