d to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2-2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2-2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2-2t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{-2(\\pi /4)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2-2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351229 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351230 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351231 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351232 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351233 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1041 "selection" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351232 13194139535478 true] [17592186351228 1110 #uuid "76ce6a6b-8469-5056-b7a6-aab2bb0f1311" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351229 1034 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi /2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535478 true] [17592186351229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13